<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Lament · War and march · 1880 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Ar soudarded 'zo gwisket e ruz</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A sad Breton song from about 1880 about a soldier from the writer's family who died in the Franco-Prussian War.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Max Ar Fur</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Bretons of the Vannes country (Gwenedeg dialect)</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Breton (Gwenedeg dialect)</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Brittany, France</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>around 1880</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Lament · War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words by Max Ar Fur (written around 1880); when they were first published is not known, so they are treated as still protected)</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ar-soudarded-zo-gwisket-e-ruz">Ar soudarded 'zo gwisket e ruz</a>&quot; (&quot;The soldiers are dressed in red&quot;) is a Breton song written around 1880 by Max Ar Fur about a relative of his who died in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>. Its words are in the Gwenedeg (Vannetais) dialect of Breton. The song's soldier, who bears the family name Ar Fur, asks to be buried in the village of Brizeux, now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vannes">Vannes</a>, if he dies at war. The music is in a minor key and in 3/4 time.</p><p>The song became well known in the 1970s through the songwriter's grandson, the singer Gweltaz Ar Fur, who sang it in his Hennebont dialect; later it was also performed in standard Breton. Other artists took it up, among them Soazig Kermabon on her 1980 album Chant et harpe celtique, Gilles Servat and Zoltán Arany, and it was included in the 2017 compilation album Breizh eo ma bro!</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A lament for a Breton soldier of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> and his attachment to his home village. Its mood is sorrowful and quiet.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so the Institute does not print them itself: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius"><div class="sg-genius-btns"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn" data-genius="4118824" data-what="Lyrics">Show the lyrics from Genius</button></div><div class="sg-genius-frames"></div><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press a button. <a href="https://genius.com/Gilles-servat-er-soudarded-zo-guisket-e-ru-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a>.</p></div></section>
</div>
